WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.900
You guys know Henry from MinutePhysics, right?

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.230
Well, he and I just made a video

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.780
on a certain quantum mechanical topic - "Bell's inequalities".

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.770
It's a really mind-warping topic

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:13.200
that not enough people know about,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.000
and even though it's a quantum thing,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.520
it's based on some surprisingly simple math,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.260
and you should definitely check it out.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:24.640
For this video, we have in mind those viewers who actually want to learn some quantum mechanics more deeply.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.700
And obviously, it's a huge topic,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.920
nowhere near the scope of a single video.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.130
But the question we asked was what topic could we present

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.380
that's not meant to be some eye-catching piece of quantum weirdness,

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.910
but which actually lays down some useful foundations

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.840
for anyone who, you know, wants to learn this field.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.820
What topic would set the right intuitions for someone

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.880
before they dove into, say, the Feynman lectures.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:51.880
Well, a natural place to start, where quantum mechanics itself started, is light.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.690
Specifically, if you want to learn quantum,

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.570
you have to have an understanding of waves, and how they're described mathematically.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.570
And what we'd like to build to here is

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:03.340
the relationship between the energy in a purely classical wave

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.240
and the probabilities that govern quantum behavior.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.550
In fact, we'll actually spend most of the time talking through

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:11.410
the pre-quantum understanding of light,

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.390
since that sets up a lot of the relevant wave mechanics.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.630
The thing is, a lot of ideas from quantum mechanics,

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:20.780
like describing states as superpositions with various amplitudes and phases,

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.140
come up in the context of classical waves

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:26.970
in a way that doesn't involve any of the quantum weirdness people might be familiar with.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.730
This also helps to appreciate what's actually different in quantum mechanics,

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:33.940
namely, certain restrictions on how much energy these waves can have,

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.190
how they behave when measured,

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:38.740
and quantum entanglement, though we won't cover entanglement in this video.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:44.770
So, we'll start with the late 1800s understanding of light as waves in the electromagnetic field.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.560
Here, let's break that down a bit.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.780
The electric field is a vector field,

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.260
and that means every point in space has some arrow attached to it,

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.720
indicating the direction and strength of the field.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.300
Now, the physical meaning of those arrows is that

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.660
if you have some charged particle in space,

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.920
there's going to be a force on that particle

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.780
in the direction of the arrow,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.590
and it's proportional to the length of the arrow and the specific charge of the particle.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.030
Likewise, the magnetic field is another vector field,

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.570
where now the physical meaning of each arrow is that

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:18.200
when a charged particle is moving through that space, there's going to be a force

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:23.100
perpendicular to both its direction of motion and to the direction of the magnetic field,

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.840
and the strength of that force is proportional to

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.560
the charge of the particle, its velocity, and the length of the magnetic field arrow.

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.740
For example, a wire with a current of moving charges

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.390
next to a magnet is either pushed or pulled by that magnetic field.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:42.180
A kind of culmination of the 19th century physics understanding of how these two fields work

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.070
are Maxwell's equations,

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:48.300
which among other things describe how each of these fields can cause a change to the other.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.090
Specifically, what Maxwell's equations tell us is that

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:55.600
when the electric field arrows seem to be forming a loop around some region,

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.280
the magnetic field will be increasing inside that region,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.190
perpendicular to the plane of the loop.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.640
And symmetrically, such a loop in the magnetic field

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.820
corresponds to a change in the electric field within it,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.520
perpendicular to the plane of the loop.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.120
Now the specifics for how exactly these equations work is really beautiful,

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.180
and worth a full video on its own.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.200
But all you need to know for now is that

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.880
one natural consequence of this mutual interplay

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.330
and how changes to one field cause changes to the other in its neighboring regions

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:27.420
is that you get these propagating waves,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.280
where the electric field and magnetic fields are oscillating

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.740
perpendicular to each other, and perpendicular to the direction of propagation.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.090
When you hear the term "electromagnetic radiation",

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:40.770
which refers to things like radio waves and visible light,

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:46.200
this is what is talking about: propagating waves in both the electric and magnetic fields.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:51.490
Of course, it's now almost mainstreamed to know of light as electromagnetic radiation,

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:54.830
but it's neat to think about just how surprising this was in Maxwell's time,

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:58.720
that these fields that have to do with forces on charged particles and magnets,

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.020
not only have something to do with light,

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.350
but that what light is is a propagating wave

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.140
as these two fields dance with each other,

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.920
causing this mutual oscillation of increasing and decreasing field strength.

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:09.320
With this as a visual,

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.820
let's take a moment to lay down the math used to describe waves.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.810
It'll still be purely classical,

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:17.260
but ideas that are core to quantum mechanics,

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.850
like superposition, amplitudes, phases,

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:22.020
all of these come up in this context,

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.600
and I would argue with a clearer motivation for what they actually mean.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.480
Take this wave and think of it as directed straight out of the screen towards your face.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.540
And let's go ahead and ignore the magnetic field right now,

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.870
just looking at how the electric field oscillates.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:41.160
And also, we're only going to focus on one of these vectors oscillating in the plane of the screen,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.180
which we'll think of as the xy plane.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.040
If it oscillates horizontally like this,

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.770
we say that the light is "horizontally polarized",

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:53.400
so the y component of this electric field is zero at all times.

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:58.600
And we might write the x component as something like cos(2πft),

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.550
where f represents some frequency, and t is time.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.390
So, if f was 1, for example,

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:10.200
that means it takes exactly 1 second for this cosine function to go through a full cycle.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.140
For a lower frequency,

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:17.660
that would mean it takes more time for the cosine to go through its full cycle.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:23.570
As the value t increases, the inside of this cosine function increases more slowly.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:28.890
Also, we're going to include another term in here - φ, called the "phase shift",

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:32.610
which tells us where this vector is in its cycle at time t=0.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.710
You'll see why that matters in just a moment.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.420
Now by default, cosine only oscillates between -1 and 1,

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:41.780
so let's put another term in front - A

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:43.860
that gives us the amplitude of this wave.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.710
One more thing, just to make things look a little more like they often do in quantum mechanics,

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.380
instead of writing it as a column vector like this,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:53.800
I'm going to separate it out into two different components

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.600
using these symbols called "kets".

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:00.120
This ket here indicates a unit vector in the horizontal direction,

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:04.750
and this ket over here represents a unit vector in the vertical direction.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:09.040
If the light is vertically polarized,

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:13.120
meaning the electric field is wiggling purely in the up-and-down direction,

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.360
its equation might look like this,

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.250
where the horizontal component is now zero,

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.220
and the vertical component is a cosine

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.090
with some frequency, amplitude, and a phase shift.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.960
Now if you have two distinct waves,

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.150
two ways of wiggling through space over time

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.080
that solve Maxwell's equations,

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.500
then adding both of these together gives another valid wave,

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:34.610
at least in a vacuum.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:36.940
That is, at each point in time,

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:40.200
add these two vectors tip-to-tail to get a new vector.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.540
Doing this at all points in space and all points in time

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.760
gives a new valid solution to Maxwell's equations,

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:48.480
at least this is all true in a vacuum.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:53.440
This is because Maxwell's equations in a vacuum are what's called "linear equations".

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.820
They're essentially a combination of derivatives acting on the electric and magnetic fields to give zero.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:01.620
So if one field F_1 satisfies this equation

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.900
and another field F_2 satisfies it,

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:09.010
then their sum F_1 plus F_2 also satisfies it, since derivatives are linear.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.690
So the sum of two or more solutions to Maxwell's equations

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.220
is also a solution to Maxwell's equations.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:20.380
This new wave is called a "superposition" of the first two.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.660
And here, superposition essentially just means sum,

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:25.920
or, in some context, weighted sum,

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.050
since if you include some kind of amplitude and phase shift in each of these components,

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:33.640
it can still be called a superposition of the two original vectors.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:39.240
Now, right now the resulting superposition is a wave wiggling in the diagonal direction.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.560
But if the horizontal and vertical components were out of phase with each other,

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.960
which might happen if you increase the phase shift in one of them,

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.980
their sum might instead trace out some sort of ellipse.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:54.720
And in the case where the phases are exactly 90° out of sync with each other,

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.500
and the amplitudes are both equal,

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.520
this is what we call "circularly polarized light".

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:01.760
This, by the way, is why it's important to

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.640
keep track not just of the amplitude in each direction,

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:09.510
but also of the phase - it affects the way that two waves add together.

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:15.400
That's also an important idea that carries over to quantum,

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:18.140
and underlies some of the things that look confusing at first.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.460
And here's another important idea.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.660
We're describing waves by adding together the horizontal and vertical components,

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:28.730
but we could also choose to describe everything with respect to different directions.

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:34.920
I mean, you could describe waves as some superposition of the diagonal and the anti-diagonal directions.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:38.260
In that case, vertically polarized light would actually be

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:42.090
a superposition of these two diagonal wiggling directions,

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:45.460
at least when both are in phase with each other, and they have the same magnitude.

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:48.420
Now, the choice of which directions you write things

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:50.500
in terms of is called a "basis".

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.250
And which basis is nicest to work with?

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.620
Well, that typically depends on what you're actually doing with the light.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:58.370
For example, if you have a polarizing filter,

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.880
like that from a set of polarized sunglasses,

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:07.280
the way these work is by absorbing the energy from electromagnetic oscillations in some particular direction.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.530
A vertically oriented polarizer, for example,

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:15.040
would absorb all of the energy from these waves along the horizontal directions,

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.280
at least classically that's how you might think about it.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:19.320
So, if you're analyzing light

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:21.200
and it's passing through a filter like this,

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:25.690
it's nice to describe it with respect to the horizontal and vertical directions.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:27.650
That way, what you can say is that

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:29.660
whatever light passes through the filter

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:32.620
is just the vertical component of the original wave.

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:39.570
But if you had a filter oriented, say diagonally,

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:43.150
well, then it would be convenient to describe things as a superposition of

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:47.180
that diagonal direction and it's perpendicular anti-diagonal direction.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:53.450
These ideas will carry over almost word-for-word to the quantum case.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.870
Quantum states, much like this wiggling direction of our wave,

00:09:56.870 --> 00:10:00.040
are described as a superposition of multiple base states,

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:02.830
where you have many choices for what base states to use.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:04.450
And just like with classical waves,

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:08.840
the components of such a superposition will have both an amplitude and a phase of some kind.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:12.240
And by the way, for those of you who do read more into quantum mechanics,

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:15.860
you'll find that these components are actually given using a single complex number

00:10:15.860 --> 00:10:17.940
rather than a cosine expression like this one.

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:19.300
One way to think of this is that

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:22.660
complex numbers are just a very convenient and natural mathematical way

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:25.820
to encode an amplitude and a phase with a single value.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:27.850
That can make things a little confusing,

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:30.540
because it's hard to visualize a pair of complex numbers,

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:33.500
which is what would describe a superposition of two base states.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:36.570
But you can think about the use of complex numbers throughout quantum mechanics

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:39.180
as a result of its underlying wavy nature

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:42.720
and its need to encapsulate the amplitude and the phase for each direction.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:46.500
Okay, just one quick point before getting into the quantum.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:47.960
Look at one of these waves,

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:51.360
and focus just on the electric field portion like we were before.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:57.630
Classically, we think about the energy of a wave like this as being proportional to the square of its amplitude.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:01.520
And I want you to notice how well this lines up with the Pythagorean theorem.

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:04.540
If you were to describe this wave as a superposition of

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:07.690
a horizontal component with amplitude A_x

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.620
and a vertical component with amplitude A_y,

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:15.730
then its energy density is proportional to (A_x)^2 plus (A_y)^2.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:18.270
And you can think of this in two different ways:

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:23.500
either it's because you're adding up the energies of each component in the superposition,

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:29.410
or it's just that you're figuring out the new amplitude using the Pythagorean theorem, and taking the square.

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:30.600
Isn't that nice?

00:11:31.540 --> 00:11:33.270
In the classical understanding of light,

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:36.930
you should be able to dial this energy up and down continuously however you want

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.710
by changing the amplitude of the wave.

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:42.970
But what physicists started to notice in the late 19th and early 20th centuries was that

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:46.060
this energy actually seems to come in discrete amounts.

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:49.400
Specifically, the energy of one of these electromagnetic waves

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:54.310
always seems to come as an integer multiple of a specific constant times the frequency of that wave.

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:56.880
We now call this constant "Planck's constant",

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.880
commonly denoting it with the letter h.

00:11:59.140 --> 00:12:00.440
Physically, what this means is that

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:03.880
whenever this wave trades its energy with something else, like an electron,

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:08.790
the amount of energy it trades off is always an integer multiple of h times its frequency.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:15.430
Importantly, this means there is some minimal non-zero energy level for waves of a given frequency - hf.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:19.000
If you have an electromagnetic wave with this frequency and energy,

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:21.980
you cannot make it smaller without eliminating it entirely.

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:23.350
That feels weird

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:26.880
when the conception of a wave is a nice continuously oscillating vector field,

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:32.220
but that's not how the universe works as late 19th and early 20th century experiments started to expose.

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:35.570
In fact, I've done a video about this, called "the origin of quantum mechanics".

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:37.660
However, it's worth noting that

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:41.880
this phenomenon is actually common in waves when they're constrained in certain ways,

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:44.070
like in pipes or instrument strings,

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:45.520
and it's called "harmonics".

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:49.300
What's weird is that electromagnetic waves do this in free space,

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:50.820
even when they're not constrained.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:55.500
And what do we call an electromagnetic wave with this minimal possible energy?

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:56.400
A photon!

00:12:56.840 --> 00:12:57.580
But like I said,

00:12:57.580 --> 00:13:01.350
the math used to describe classical electromagnetic waves

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:03.420
carries over to describing a photon.

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:07.320
It might have, say, a 45° diagonal polarization,

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:12.400
which can be described as a superposition of a purely horizontal state and a purely vertical state,

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:15.690
where each one of these components has some amplitude and phase.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.500
And with a different choice of basis,

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:21.690
that same state might be described as a superposition of two other directions.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:26.020
All of this is stuff that you would see if you started reading more into quantum mechanics,

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:29.860
but this superposition has a different interpretation than before,

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:31.510
and it has to.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:35.890
Let's say you were thinking of this diagonally polarized photon kind of classically,

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:39.840
and you said it has an amplitude of 1 unit for some appropriate unit system.

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:46.560
Well, that would make the hypothetical amplitudes of its horizontal and vertical components each √(1/2).

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:48.050
and like Henry said,

00:13:48.050 --> 00:13:52.120
the energy of a photon is this special constant h times its frequency.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:54.190
And because in a classical setting,

00:13:54.190 --> 00:13:57.400
energy is proportional to the square of the amplitude of this wave,

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.460
it's tempting to think of

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:02.410
half of the energy as being in the horizontal component,

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:04.850
and half of it as being in the vertical component.

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:09.340
But waves of this frequency cannot have half the energy of a photon.

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:12.180
I mean, the whole novelty of quantum here is that

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.280
energy comes in these discrete indivisible chunks.

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:21.780
So these components with an imagined amplitude of 1/√2 could not exist in isolation,

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:24.560
and you might wonder what exactly they mean.

00:14:25.510 --> 00:14:27.520
Well, let's get experimental about it.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:31.360
If you were to take a vertically oriented polarizing filter,

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:34.810
and shoot this diagonally polarized photon right at it,

00:14:35.210 --> 00:14:36.250
what do you think would happen?

00:14:37.720 --> 00:14:40.940
Classically, the way you'd interpret the superposition is that

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:44.720
the half of its energy in the horizontal direction would be absorbed,

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:48.340
but because energy comes in these discrete photon packets,

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:50.660
it either has to pass through with all of its energy,

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:52.260
or get absorbed entirely.

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:54.710
And if you actually did this experiment,

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:57.700
about half the time the photon goes through entirely,

00:14:57.700 --> 00:15:00.280
and about half the time it gets absorbed entirely,

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:04.580
and it appears to be random whether a given photon passes through or not.

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:07.360
If it does pass through,

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:10.560
forcing it to make a decision like this actually changes it

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:14.240
so that it's polarization is oriented along the filter's direction.

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:17.740
This is analogous to the classic Schrodinger's cat setup:

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:20.490
you have something that's in a superposition of two states,

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:23.700
but once you make a measurement of that superposition,

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.720
forcing it to interact with an observer,

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:28.680
in a way where each of those two states would behave differently.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:30.980
From the perspective of that observer,

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:36.260
this superposition collapses to be entirely in one state, or entirely in another:

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:37.630
dead or alive,

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:39.100
horizontal or vertical.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:42.080
One pretty neat way to see this in action,

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:44.560
which Henry and I talked about in the other video,

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:48.970
is to take several polarized sunglasses or some other form of polarizing filters

00:15:49.220 --> 00:15:52.540
and start by holding two of them between you and some light source.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:56.070
If you rotate them to be 90° off from each other,

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.220
the light source is blacked out completely.

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:00.410
(or at least with perfect filters it would be)

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:04.960
Because all of the photons passing through that first one are polarized vertically,

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:09.650
so they actually have a 0% chance of passing a filter oriented horizontally.

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:15.760
But if you insert a third filter oriented at a 45° angle between the two,

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:18.180
it actually lets more light through!

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:20.170
And what's going on here is that

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:25.700
50% of the photons passing that vertical filter will also pass through the diagonal filter.

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:31.020
And once they do, they're going to be changed to have a purely diagonal polarization.

00:16:31.380 --> 00:16:32.790
And then once they're in that state,

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:37.100
they have a 50/50 chance of passing through the filter oriented at 90°.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:40.290
So even though 0% of the photons passing through

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:43.700
the first would pass through that last if nothing was in between,

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:46.000
by introducing another filter,

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:48.480
25% of them now passed through all three.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:51.180
Now that's something that you could not explain

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:55.020
unless that middle filter forces the photons to change their states.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:56.930
And that experiment, by the way,

00:16:56.930 --> 00:17:01.020
becomes all the weirder when you dig into the specific probabilities

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:03.580
for angles between 0 and 45°,

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:06.550
and that's actually what we talked about in the other video.

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:10.110
For example, one specific value we focus on there is

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:17.100
the probability that a photon whose polarization is 22.5° off the direction of a filter

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:19.340
is going to end up passing through that filter.

00:17:19.990 --> 00:17:23.630
Again, it's helpful to think of this wave as having an amplitude of 1,

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:29.320
and then you think of the horizontal component as having amplitude sin(22.5°),

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:31.210
which is around 0.38;

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:36.260
and the vertical component would have an amplitude cos(22.5°),

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:38.040
which is around 0.92.

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:47.600
Classically, you might think of its horizontal component as having energy proportional to 0.38^2,

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:49.350
which is around 0.15.

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:55.270
Likewise, you might think of the vertical component as having an energy proportional to 0.92^2,

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:57.670
which comes out to be around 0.85.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.670
And like we said before, classically, this would mean,

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:02.800
if you pass it through a vertical filter,

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:06.760
15% of its energy is absorbed in the horizontal direction,

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:10.360
but because the energy of light comes in these discrete quanta

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:12.380
that cannot be subdivided,

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:14.180
instead, what you observe is that

00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:17.820
85% of the time the photon passes through entirely,

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:20.900
and 15% of the time it gets completely blocked.

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:29.040
Now I want to emphasize that the wave equations don't change.

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:33.420
The photon is still described as a superposition of two oscillating components,

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:35.370
each with some phase and amplitude,

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:38.280
and these are often encoded using a single complex number.

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:39.630
The difference is that

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:42.930
classically the squares of the amplitudes of each component

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:46.230
tells you the amount of that wave's energy in each direction;

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:48.040
but with quantized light,

00:18:48.040 --> 00:18:50.580
at this minimal non-zero energy level,

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.720
the squares of those amplitudes tell you the probabilities

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:58.900
that a given photon is going to be found to have all of its energy in one direction or not.

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:02.660
Also, these components could still have some kind of phase difference.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:06.660
Just like with classical waves, photons can be circularly polarized,

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:11.790
and there exists polarizing filters that only let through photons that are polarized circularly,

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:13.580
say in the clockwise direction.

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:17.850
Or rather, they let through all photons probabilistically,

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:19.670
where the probabilities are determined

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:25.690
by describing each one of those photons as a superposition of the clockwise and counterclockwise states,

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:30.880
and then the square of the amplitude of the clockwise component gives you the desired probability.

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:34.900
Photons are, of course, just one quantum phenomenon,

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:38.050
one where we initially understood it as a wave thanks to Maxwell's equations,

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:40.480
and then as individual particles or quanta -

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:42.350
hence, the name "quantum mechanics".

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:45.470
But as many of you well know, there's a flipside to this,

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:49.240
where are many things that were understood to come in discrete little packets, like electrons,

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:52.460
were discovered to be governed by similar wavy quantum mechanics.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:56.820
In cases way more general than this one photon polarization example,

00:19:56.820 --> 00:20:01.100
quantum mechanical states are described as some superposition of multiple base states,

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.640
and the superposition depends on what basis you choose.

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:07.640
Each component in this superposition is given with an amplitude and a phase

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:10.040
often encoded as a single complex number.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:13.690
And the need for this phase arises from the wave nature of these objects.

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:15.500
As with the photon example,

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:19.150
the choice of how to measure these objects can determine a set of base states,

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:22.400
where the probability of measuring a particle to be in one of these base states

00:20:22.400 --> 00:20:25.430
is proportional to the squares of the amplitudes of these numbers.

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:26.740
It's funny to think, though,

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:30.990
that if the wavy nature of electrons and other particles was discovered first,

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:35.060
we might instead refer to the whole subject as "harmonic mechanics", or something like that,

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:36.130
since the weirdness there

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.190
is not that waves come in discrete units,

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:41.010
but that particles are governed by wave equations.

